//! The Modbus register map as data: **map version 2**, the node model of
//! ADR 0004 decision 7.
//!
//! The ADR calls this the contract the future RS-485 "duck" protocol
//! reuses, so the table lives here (one source), the server in
//! granite-fw reads values through [`RegisterImage`] (which asks this
//! module for one address at a time), writes come back as a [`Command`]
//! through [`command_from_holding`] / [`command_from_coil`], and
//! `firmware/docs/modbus_map.md` is generated by [`render_markdown`].
//!
//! Version 2 is a deliberate break with the rev C map (version 1): a
//! controller serves up to 64 nodes on up to 8 planes, which no longer
//! fits in a flat block of eight. What did not move: the fixed
//! controller registers, and the map version in input register
//! [`MAP_VERSION_ADDR`], so a client can read the version first and then
//! decide.
//!
//! The map is sparse. Everything that is not in it answers exception 2
//! (illegal data address), including the rev C node registers: a client
//! written for version 1 fails loudly instead of reading a plausible
//! wrong node.
//!
//! ```text
//! Discrete inputs    8-13      dry inputs, link, MQTT
//!                    100+id-1  node rail on
//!                    200+id-1  node host on
//! Input registers    0-16      probes, board temp, VIN, uptime, fw,
//!                              faults, map version, planes
//!                    100+      per node, 12 registers (NODE_STRIDE)
//!                    900+      per plane, 16 registers (PLANE_STRIDE)
//! Holding registers  8         on_all trigger
//!                    9         last command result
//!                    100+id-1  node command word
//!                    200+p     plane command word
//! Coils              100+id-1  node rail, read and write
//! ```

